{
 "segments": [
  {
   "text": "Case Nineteen.",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 1.923,
   "pause": 0.6
  },
  {
   "text": "The Ship's Bell.",
   "start": 2.5229999999999997,
   "end": 3.547,
   "pause": 1.4
  },
  {
   "text": "Above the door of the sailing club hangs a brass ship's bell, rung for the start of every race. It hangs on a hook so high above the door that, as everyone in Tidewhistle Cove likes to say, even the tallest person in the village has to stretch up on tiptoe to reach it.",
   "start": 4.947,
   "end": 21.609,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "The tallest person in the village is Tobias Ferris, who is on the lifeboat crew. He is six and a half feet tall and has to duck through most doorways.",
   "start": 22.459,
   "end": 31.632,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "The club's stepladder lives in a cupboard that is kept locked, and Agnes Bell, who is the club treasurer, keeps the only key on her own key ring. On Sunday the cupboard was still locked, and the ladder was still inside. There was no other furniture in the porch to stand on, only a tiny wooden stool that had been broken for months.",
   "start": 32.482,
   "end": 52.322,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "On Sunday morning, the bell was gone from its hook.",
   "start": 53.172,
   "end": 56.073,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Three people had been at the club on Saturday evening for the quiz night.",
   "start": 56.923,
   "end": 60.849,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Loveday Nance is a little over five feet tall. She said she had been at the quiz table all evening, and that she couldn't reach that hook if she stood on a chair.",
   "start": 61.699,
   "end": 70.637,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Mr Fenwick is not much taller than Loveday. He said he had left early with a headache.",
   "start": 71.487,
   "end": 76.479,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Tobias said he had been at the quiz too, and had gone home at ten.",
   "start": 77.329,
   "end": 80.849,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "\"I didn't touch the bell,\" said Tobias. \"I hardly ever even notice it.\"",
   "start": 81.699,
   "end": 86.073,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Agnes jingled her key ring and looked up at the empty hook.",
   "start": 86.923,
   "end": 90.421,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Can you solve it? Who took the ship's bell?",
   "start": 91.521,
   "end": 93.847,
   "pause": 1.2
  },
  {
   "text": "Think about it.",
   "start": 95.047,
   "end": 96.071,
   "pause": 0.9
  },
  {
   "text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
   "start": 96.971,
   "end": 102.197,
   "pause": 4.5
  },
  {
   "text": "The Solution.",
   "start": 106.697,
   "end": 107.721,
   "pause": 1.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Tobias Ferris took the bell.",
   "start": 108.821,
   "end": 110.571,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "The hook is so high that even the tallest person in the village has to stretch on tiptoe to reach it, and the tallest person in the village is Tobias. Loveday and Mr Fenwick are both much shorter, and there was nothing they could have stood on. The stepladder was locked in the cupboard, Agnes had the only key, and the stool was broken. Only Tobias could have lifted the bell from its hook.",
   "start": 111.421,
   "end": 134.098,
   "pause": 0.85
  },
  {
   "text": "Tobias went red. The bell had a crack, he explained, and it had been clanking instead of ringing for weeks. He had taken it to his workshop to mend it as a surprise for the club, and he hadn't wanted to spoil the surprise by admitting it. The next Saturday the bell was back on its hook, and it rang out as clear as a church bell. The sailing club gave Tobias a round of applause, and he had to duck to get out of the room.",
   "start": 134.948,
   "end": 159.097,
   "pause": 0.0
  }
 ],
